3800. Minimum cost to make 2 binary strs equal
 
/*
Step 1: Focus only on mismatches
 
If s[i] === t[i], do nothing.
Only positions where s[i] !== t[i] matter.
 
Step 2: Notice there are ONLY two mismatch types
 
At any mismatched index, only two cases exist:
 
s = 0 , t = 1 → call this type c0
 
s = 1 , t = 0 → call this type c1
 
This is the key insight.
Everything depends on how many c0 and c1 you have.
 
Step 3: Understand what each operation can fix
Operation   What it fixes
Flip    Fixes 1 mismatch
Swap    Fixes 1 c0 + 1 c1
Cross swap  Helps when mismatches are unbalanced
 
So the problem becomes:
 
How do I optimally reduce c0 and c1 to zero at minimum cost?
 
Step 4: Pair what can be paired
 
If you have both c0 and c1, pair them
 
Each pair can be fixed with one swap
 
That’s why:
 
min(c0, c1) * swapCost
 
Step 5: Deal with leftovers
 
After pairing:
 
If mismatches remain → they are all same type
 
Options:
 
Flip individually
 
Use cross swaps (sometimes cheaper)
 
That’s why the code tries multiple strategies and picks the minimum.
 
🔹 Why the solution checks multiple results
 
Because no single operation is always optimal:
 
Sometimes flips are cheapest
 
Sometimes swaps dominate
 
Sometimes cross + swap reduces cost
 
So the code:
 
tries all meaningful combinations and chooses the cheapest
*/
 
/**
 * @param {string} s
 * @param {string} t
 * @param {number} flipCost
 * @param {number} swapCost
 * @param {number} crossCost
 * @return {number}
 */
var minimumCost = function(s, t, flipCost, swapCost, crossCost) {
    let c0=0;
    let c1=0;
    for(let i=0;i<s.length;i++){
        if(s[i]!==t[i]){
            if(s[i]==='0')c0++
            else c1++;
        }    
    }
    let r1=(c0+c1)*flipCost;
    let r2=Math.min(c0,c1)*swapCost+Math.abs(c0-c1)*flipCost;
    let diff=Math.abs(c0-c1);
    let r3=Math.min(c0,c1)*swapCost+
            Math.floor(diff/2)*(swapCost+crossCost)+
            (diff%2)*flipCost;
    return Math.min(r1,r2,r3);
 
};
